"""Add Android games (.apk) to Steam, set to run with Lepton (Kettle).

  kettle-android-games inspect FILE          what the APK is and whether it can run here
  kettle-android-games add [--notify] FILE   copy it to ~/Games/Android/<package>/ and add a
                                             Steam shortcut using "Lepton (Kettle)"
  kettle-android-games list                  the added games (name, size, whether in Steam)
  kettle-android-games remove PACKAGE        remove a game: its Steam shortcut, files, saves
                                             and Lepton's container for it
  kettle-android-games fdroid-search TEXT    search F-Droid
  kettle-android-games fdroid-add PACKAGE    download an F-Droid app and add it
  kettle-android-games play-status           whether a Google account is signed in
  kettle-android-games play-signin [EMAIL] [TOKEN]
                                             sign in to Google with the one-time oauth_token
                                             from accounts.google.com/EmbeddedSetup; without a
                                             token, it's taken from Firefox after the user
                                             signed in there
  kettle-android-games play-signout          forget the Google account
  kettle-android-games play-search TEXT      search Google Play
  kettle-android-games play-add APP          download a game from Google Play (its link or
                                             package name) and add it

Every command prints one JSON object ({"ok": true, ...} or {"ok": false, "error": ...}) and
exits 0 or 1. While downloading, fdroid-add and play-add report "PROGRESS <bytes> <total bytes>"
lines on stderr, a few times a second. Shortcuts are added through the running Steam client (its CEF remote debugging
port, which Kettle enables for Decky), so Steam must be running.

.xapk and .apks files are unpacked: the base APK, its split APKs (an app bundle's native code,
screen density and language parts) and OBB data. Splits go to splits/ next to the game's APK,
where Lepton (Kettle) installs them together with it.

The Google Play download is opt-in and unofficial: it uses apkeep with the Google account the
user signs in with, and the device profile of an Android 11 arm64 phone so Google Play hands
out builds Lepton's Android 11 can run. Signing in is apkeep's documented way: the user signs
in on Google's embedded setup page in Firefox, which sets a one-time oauth_token cookie; it's
traded once for a long-lived token, kept in ~/.config/kettle-lepton/google-play.ini. Rather
than have the user copy the cookie out of Firefox's developer tools (hard on a handheld),
play-signin reads it from the Firefox profile: cookies.sqlite, or, as Google's is a session
cookie, the session store Firefox writes every 15 seconds. The account's email comes from
Firefox's form history of Google's sign-in box, unless given.
"""
